1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,349
This is ECE 358, video 25 a. We solve the first half of the new handout: Selected Problems from Previous Midterm Exams.

2
00:00:11,799 --> 00:00:14,914
First, question 1: message segmentation.

3
00:00:15,364 --> 00:00:19,652
Then question 4: a file over four store-and-forward links.

4
00:00:20,102 --> 00:00:25,371
Then question 3: a sliding window on one link, and stop-and-wait on the next.

5
00:00:25,821 --> 00:00:34,867
The handout gives official answers, and all our answers match them. Video 25 b solves questions 2, 5 and 6.

6
00:00:35,317 --> 00:00:57,034
Question 1 is about message segmentation. A source sends a message to a destination through two packet switches. So the path has 3 links, and each link is 2 megabits per second. The switches use store-and-forward. We ignore propagation, queueing and processing delays, so only the transmission delay is left.

7
00:00:57,484 --> 00:01:06,145
In part a, the source sends the message of 8 megabits as one unit. Find the time to the first switch, and the total time.

8
00:01:06,595 --> 00:01:13,443
In parts b and c, the source cuts the message into 800 packets of 10,000 bits each.

9
00:01:13,893 --> 00:01:29,189
Check: 800 times 10,000 bits is 8 times 10 to the 6 bits, the same 8 megabits. Find the times for the first two packets, and the total time. Then compare with part a. Pause the video and try it.

10
00:01:29,639 --> 00:01:41,949
Part a. First, write the given values in base units. 8 megabits is 8 times 10 to the 6 bits. 2 megabits per second is 2 times 10 to the 6 bits per second.

11
00:01:42,399 --> 00:01:59,465
The source pushes all 8 megabits onto link 1. The transmission delay is L over R: 8 times 10 to the 6, divided by 2 times 10 to the 6. That is 4 seconds. So switch 1 has the whole message at 4 seconds.

12
00:01:59,915 --> 00:02:09,238
Switch 1 uses store-and-forward. It sends only after it has all 8 megabits. So link 2 is busy from 4 to 8 seconds.

13
00:02:09,688 --> 00:02:17,325
Switch 2 does the same. Link 3 is busy from 8 to 12 seconds. At any time, only one link is busy.

14
00:02:17,775 --> 00:02:30,405
So the destination has the message after 3 times 4 seconds, which is 12 seconds. This matches the official answer: 4 seconds to the first switch, and 12 seconds in total.

15
00:02:30,855 --> 00:02:46,791
Part b. One packet has 10,000 bits, that is 10 to the 4 bits. Its transmission delay on one link is 10 to the 4, divided by 2 times 10 to the 6. That is 5 times 10 to the minus 3 seconds.

16
00:02:47,241 --> 00:02:55,347
5 times 10 to the minus 3 seconds is 5 milliseconds. So switch 1 has packet 1 at 5 milliseconds.

17
00:02:55,797 --> 00:03:03,627
From 5 to 10 milliseconds, two links are busy at the same time. Switch 1 sends packet 1 on link 2.

18
00:03:04,077 --> 00:03:07,767
At the same time, the source sends packet 2 on link 1.

19
00:03:08,217 --> 00:03:19,332
So switch 1 has all of packet 2 at 2 times 5, which is 10 milliseconds. This matches the official answer: 5 milliseconds and 10 milliseconds.

20
00:03:19,782 --> 00:03:31,579
Part c. Packet 1 crosses 3 links, with 5 milliseconds on each link. 3 times 5 is 15. So the destination has packet 1 at 15 milliseconds.

21
00:03:32,029 --> 00:03:42,867
The links work in parallel. Each link sends one packet every 5 milliseconds. So packet 2 arrives at 15 plus 5, that is 20 milliseconds.

22
00:03:43,317 --> 00:03:51,253
Packets 3 and 4 arrive at 25 and 30 milliseconds, and so on: one packet every 5 milliseconds.

23
00:03:51,703 --> 00:03:59,447
Packet 800 arrives 800 minus 1, that is 799 packet times after packet 1.

24
00:03:59,897 --> 00:04:15,470
15 plus 799 times 5 is 15 plus 3995, which is 4010 milliseconds. That is 4.01 seconds. This matches the official answer.

25
00:04:15,920 --> 00:04:37,211
Video 3 gives a general rule for store-and-forward. The time is the number of links, plus the number of packets, minus 1, times one packet time. 3 plus 800 minus 1 is 802. 802 times 5 milliseconds is 4010 milliseconds. The result is the same.

26
00:04:37,661 --> 00:04:48,455
Here is the busy time of each link, to scale. In part a, the links work one after the other. Each link is busy for 4 seconds. The total is 12 seconds.

27
00:04:48,905 --> 00:05:01,215
In part c, all three links are busy at almost the same time. Each link starts only 5 milliseconds after the link before it. The total is 4.01 seconds.

28
00:05:01,665 --> 00:05:08,214
4.01 divided by 12 is 0.334, about one third.

29
00:05:08,664 --> 00:05:21,016
So segmentation needs only about one third of the time, because the links work in parallel. This is the official comment: the delay is significantly less, almost one third.

30
00:05:21,466 --> 00:05:37,189
Question 4. The network has four packet switches, S1 to S4, and six hosts. Every link is 1 megabit per second. The switches use store-and-forward. We ignore processing, propagation and queueing delays.

31
00:05:37,639 --> 00:05:46,193
Host A sends a file of 1 megabyte to host F. The packets are 1 kilobyte each. Packet 1 starts at time 0.

32
00:05:46,643 --> 00:05:52,595
First, find the path. A goes to S1, then to S2, then to S4, then to F.

33
00:05:53,045 --> 00:05:58,677
That is 4 links, all with the same rate. Pause the video and try parts a to d.

34
00:05:59,127 --> 00:06:11,287
Part a. One packet is 1 kilobyte. The official solution uses 1 kilobyte equal to 1000 bytes. 1000 bytes times 8 bits is 8000 bits.

35
00:06:11,737 --> 00:06:22,660
The transmission delay on one link is 8000 divided by 10 to the 6. That is 8 times 10 to the minus 3 seconds, or 8 milliseconds.

36
00:06:23,110 --> 00:06:31,793
Each switch stores the whole packet, and then sends it on the next link. So the packet needs 8 milliseconds on each of the 4 links.

37
00:06:32,243 --> 00:06:40,328
So host F has packet 1 at 4 times 8, which is 32 milliseconds. This matches the official answer.

38
00:06:40,778 --> 00:06:51,445
Part b. The number of packets is 1 megabyte divided by 1 kilobyte. 10 to the 6, divided by 10 to the 3, is 1000 packets.

39
00:06:51,895 --> 00:06:56,439
From part a, packet 1 arrives at F at 32 milliseconds.

40
00:06:56,889 --> 00:07:07,107
The 4 links work in parallel. So after packet 1, one packet arrives every 8 milliseconds: at 40 and 48 milliseconds, and so on.

41
00:07:07,557 --> 00:07:19,909
Packet 1000 arrives 999 packet times after packet 1. 999 times 8 is 7992 milliseconds.

42
00:07:20,359 --> 00:07:29,362
32 plus 7992 is 8024 milliseconds. This matches the official answer.

43
00:07:29,812 --> 00:07:44,553
Part c. The average throughput is the file size divided by the total time. The file is 10 to the 6 bytes. The time is 8024 milliseconds, which is 8.024 seconds.

44
00:07:45,003 --> 00:07:59,062
10 to the 6 divided by 8.024 is about 124,626 bytes per second. That is 124.63 kilobytes per second.

45
00:07:59,512 --> 00:08:13,229
Multiply by 8 bits per byte. The result is about 997,009 bits per second, or 997 kilobits per second. This matches the official answer.

46
00:08:13,679 --> 00:08:26,501
This is a little less than the link rate of 1 megabit per second. In the first 24 milliseconds, 3 times 8, no bits arrive at F. Packet 1 is still on the first three links.

47
00:08:26,951 --> 00:08:36,295
After that, F receives bits at 1 megabit per second for 8000 milliseconds: 1000 packets times 8 milliseconds.

48
00:08:36,745 --> 00:08:50,014
Part d uses the bottleneck link. The bottleneck is the link with the smallest rate on the path. Here all 4 links have the same rate, 1 megabit per second. So any of them is the bottleneck.

49
00:08:50,464 --> 00:08:54,859
The estimate: the whole file must cross the bottleneck link at its rate.

50
00:08:55,309 --> 00:09:10,583
The file is 8 times 10 to the 6 bits. At 10 to the 6 bits per second, that is 8 seconds: 1000 packets times 8 milliseconds, or 8000 milliseconds. This matches the official answer.

51
00:09:11,033 --> 00:09:28,655
Compare with the exact time of part b, 8024 milliseconds. The difference is 24 milliseconds. That is 3 times 8 milliseconds: the time that packet 1 needs on the other 3 links. For a large file, this difference is small.

52
00:09:29,105 --> 00:09:46,321
Question 3. Node A sends frames to node C through node B. Link A B is 200 kilometers long and runs at 1 megabit per second. Link B C is 100 kilometers long. Its rate, R B C, is the unknown.

53
00:09:46,771 --> 00:09:58,099
The propagation speed is 10 to the 8 meters per second on both lines. Data frames are 1000 bits. ACKs have negligible length, and there are no errors.

54
00:09:58,549 --> 00:10:13,034
Frames arrive at B over link A B, and they leave over link B C. The buffers of B do not flood if frames leave at least as fast as they arrive. So the throughput of B C must be at least the throughput of A B.

55
00:10:13,484 --> 00:10:29,249
Link A B uses a sliding window: 2 in part a, and 10 in part b. Link B C uses stop-and-wait. Video 9 solves a similar problem, problem 3 of problem set 3. Pause the video and try it.

56
00:10:29,699 --> 00:10:48,643
Part a, link A B first. The propagation delay is 200 kilometers divided by 10 to the 8 meters per second. 200 kilometers is 2 times 10 to the 5 meters. So d prop is 2 times 10 to the minus 3 seconds, or 2 milliseconds.

57
00:10:49,093 --> 00:11:00,635
The transmission delay of one frame is 1000 bits divided by 10 to the 6 bits per second. That is 10 to the minus 3 seconds, or 1 millisecond.

58
00:11:01,085 --> 00:11:21,842
The last bit of frame 1 arrives at B at 1 plus 2, that is 3 milliseconds. B sends ACK 1 at once. The ACK has negligible length, so it arrives at A at 3 plus 2, that is 5 milliseconds. So one cycle, T A B, is d trans plus 2 d prop, which is 5 milliseconds.

59
00:11:22,292 --> 00:11:36,393
The window is 2. So A sends frame 2 right after frame 1, from 1 to 2 milliseconds. Then the window is full, and A must wait for ACK 1. A is idle for 5 minus 2, that is 3 milliseconds.

60
00:11:36,843 --> 00:11:46,849
ACK 1 arrives at 5 milliseconds, and A sends frame 3. The pattern repeats: A is busy for 2 milliseconds of every 5 milliseconds.

61
00:11:47,299 --> 00:11:57,667
So the utilization is the minimum of W times d trans over T A B, and 1. 2 times 1, divided by 5, is 0.4.

62
00:11:58,117 --> 00:12:10,981
The throughput is the utilization times the link rate. 0.4 times 10 to the 6 is 4 times 10 to the 5 bits per second, or 400 kilobits per second.

63
00:12:11,431 --> 00:12:23,825
Each frame has 1000 bits. 4 times 10 to the 5, divided by 1000, is 400 frames per second into B. This matches the official solution.

64
00:12:24,275 --> 00:12:39,891
Now link B C. The propagation delay is 100 kilometers divided by 10 to the 8 meters per second. 10 to the 5, divided by 10 to the 8, is 10 to the minus 3 seconds. That is 1 millisecond.

65
00:12:40,341 --> 00:12:51,840
The transmission delay is 1000 bits divided by the unknown rate R B C. The figure draws it with the answer of part a, 0.5 milliseconds.

66
00:12:52,290 --> 00:13:06,242
With stop-and-wait, B sends one frame and then waits for its ACK. The wait is 2 d prop, that is 2 milliseconds. So the cycle, T B C, is d trans plus 0.002 seconds.

67
00:13:06,692 --> 00:13:17,337
Only then does B send the next frame. So link B C carries one frame per cycle. Its throughput is 1 over T B C, in frames per second.

68
00:13:17,787 --> 00:13:27,025
The green curve shows the throughput of B C against its rate. A faster link makes the transmission delay smaller, so the throughput grows.

69
00:13:27,475 --> 00:13:43,389
But the 2 milliseconds of propagation in each cycle stay. So even with an infinite rate, d trans goes to 0, and B C carries at most 1 over 0.002. That is 500 frames per second.

70
00:13:43,839 --> 00:13:54,997
In part a, 400 frames per second arrive at B. B is not flooded if 1 over d trans plus 0.002 is at least 400.

71
00:13:55,447 --> 00:14:05,047
So d trans plus 0.002 must be at most 1 over 400. That is 0.0025 seconds.

72
00:14:05,497 --> 00:14:17,038
So d trans must be at most 0.0025 minus 0.002. That is 0.0005 seconds.

73
00:14:17,488 --> 00:14:39,867
1000 bits over R B C must be at most 0.0005 seconds. So R B C must be at least 1000 divided by 0.0005. That is 2 times 10 to the 6 bits per second, or 2 megabits per second. This matches the official answer.

74
00:14:40,317 --> 00:14:55,079
Part b. Link A B now has a window of 10. The delays are the same: 1 millisecond of transmission, and a cycle of 5 milliseconds. In one cycle, A can send 5 over 1, that is 5 frames.

75
00:14:55,529 --> 00:15:05,364
ACK 1 arrives at 5 milliseconds, just when frame 5 ends. The window still has room, so A sends frame 6 at once. A is never idle.

76
00:15:05,814 --> 00:15:15,478
The ideal window size for full utilization is T A B over d trans, which is 5. The window, 10, is larger than 5.

77
00:15:15,928 --> 00:15:24,611
So the utilization is the minimum of 10 times 1 over 5, and 1. That is the minimum of 2 and 1, which is 1.

78
00:15:25,061 --> 00:15:35,343
The throughput is 1 times 10 to the 6 bits per second. Divided by 1000 bits per frame, that is 1000 frames per second into B.

79
00:15:35,793 --> 00:15:45,329
Link B C still uses stop-and-wait. Its throughput is 1 over d trans plus 0.002, as in part a.

80
00:15:45,779 --> 00:15:59,305
The largest value comes when the link is very fast, so d trans is close to 0. Then the throughput is 1 over 0.002, which is 500 frames per second.

81
00:15:59,755 --> 00:16:05,984
But 1000 frames per second arrive at B. 500 is less than 1000.

82
00:16:06,434 --> 00:16:20,173
So no rate of B C is enough. The buffers of B flood for every value of R B C. The cause is the propagation delay of B C, not its rate. This matches the official answer.

83
00:16:20,623 --> 00:16:31,887
Here is the recap. One. With store-and-forward, a node sends a packet only after it has all of it. One message over n links takes n times L over R.

84
00:16:32,337 --> 00:16:39,931
Two. k packets over n links take n plus k minus 1 packet times, because the links work in parallel.

85
00:16:40,381 --> 00:16:51,603
Three. The average throughput is the file size divided by the total time. The bottleneck estimate is the file size divided by the smallest rate on the path.

86
00:16:52,053 --> 00:17:06,026
Four. A sliding window has a utilization of W times d trans over d trans plus 2 d prop, at most 1. Stop-and-wait sends one frame per d trans plus 2 d prop.

87
00:17:06,476 --> 00:17:19,873
Five. A node is not flooded if its output throughput is at least its input throughput. That is the end of video 25 a. Video 25 b solves questions 2, 5 and 6.
